digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56776-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to investigate whether post-COVID-19 resurgences of common respiratory viruses show globally consistent or region-specific timing patterns by compiling worldwide surveillance data, defining epidemic onsets uniformly, and using generalized linear mixed-effects models to compare onset-to-onset and peak-to-peak intervals across viruses and locations.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A global, multi-source comparison using standardized epidemic timing definitions and mixed-effects interval modeling can test whether observed post-pandemic respiratory-virus resurgences are shared globally or differ by region."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Across different data sources, epidemic onsets were identified using a unified 4-week trend method in which four consecutive weeks of higher activity than the corresponding weeks four weeks earlier marked onset."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The analysis used descriptive pooling and analytical matching approaches together with a generalized linear mixed-effects model framework to synthesize intervals between epidemic onsets and between epidemic peaks."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The study combined worldwide surveillance and collaborator data from multiple sources with a unified 4-week trend approach for identifying epidemic onsets."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Using the compiled multi-source respiratory-virus dataset and standardized epidemic-onset identification, the study applied generalized linear mixed-effects modeling to compare onset-to-onset and peak-to-peak timing intervals."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because it remained unclear whether post-COVID-19 respiratory-virus resurgence timing was globally consistent or region-specific, the compiled worldwide dataset, unified epidemic-onset definitions, and GLMM-based interval comparisons provide an appropriate study design to investigate those patterns."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Prior reports indicate that common respiratory viruses, including IV, RSV, PIV, MPV, seasonal coronaviruses, and RV, have distinct seasonal circulation patterns, with influenza winter epidemics in temperate sites illustrating that these patterns can vary by virus and geography."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because large-scale NPIs reduce transmission and activity of respiratory viruses, the observed major disruption of common respiratory-virus circulation in 2020 is plausibly explained in part by the COVID-19 pandemic response and associated NPIs."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Given that common respiratory viruses have virus- and geography-dependent seasonal circulation patterns and are important causes of severe respiratory infections, their post-COVID-19 resurgence timing warrants study as a potentially region-specific rather than uniformly global phenomenon."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Observed asynchronous resurgences of common respiratory viruses, together with prior evidence of virus- and geography-dependent circulation patterns, make it unclear whether post-COVID-19 resurgence timing reflects globally consistent or region-specific patterns."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The study assembled worldwide respiratory-virus activity data by combining published studies, online surveillance reports, and international collaborator data, including the RSV GEN network."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Induction-case: Human respiratory viruses such as influenza virus (IV) and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) are important causes of severe respiratory infections."];
  "n14" [label="(5,0,0) induction: Given the induction-case that According to surveillance in New South Wales Australia, while all viruses lower in 2020, RV and AdV least affected, RSV suppressed until out-of-season in Sep 2020, PIV and MPV limited until mid-2021, IV low by end 2021 and the induction-common that Similar asynchronous resurgence was also noted in other countries with varied timing and stridency of NPIs, therefore the resurgence of these common respiratory viruses did not occur simultaneously."];
  "n19" [label="(9,0,0) Induction-case: In this study, we compiled a large dataset on respiratory virus activity across the globe from published studies, surveillance reports, and international collaborators."];
  "n2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Causes of severe respiratory infections require monitoring of their circulation patterns for public health preparedness."];
  "n20" [label="(12,0,0) Induction-common: Data were collected from three sources: systematic literature review, online surveillance reports, and the RSV GEN network."];
  "n22" [label="(13,0,0) Induction-case: The epidemic onset was defined using a “4-week trend” method, where each week’s viral activity (case numbers or positive proportion) was compared to that of four weeks prior."];
  "n23" [label="(14,0,0) Induction-common: An epidemic onset was identified when four consecutive weeks showed higher activity than their respective weeks four weeks earlier (“up-weeks”)."];
  "n24" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given the induction-case that The epidemic onset was defined using a “4-week trend” method, where each week’s viral activity (case numbers or positive proportion) was compared to that of four weeks prior and the induction-common that An epidemic onset was identified when four consecutive weeks showed higher activity than their respective weeks four weeks earlier, therefore the epidemic onset was defined using the 4-week trend method with identification when four consecutive weeks showed higher activity."];
  "n25" [label="(10,0,0) Induction-case: We determined the onset and peak of epidemics from different data sources using a unified method."];
  "n27" [label="(15,0,0) Induction-case: Two approaches were implemented: (1) A descriptive pooling approach that compared intervals between consecutive epidemic onsets of the same virus across sites for both pre- and post-pandemic periods; (2) An analytical matching approach that examined the relative timing differences between pairs of viruses’ resurgence, matched by study site."];
  "n28" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: A generalised linear mixed-effects model (GLMM) framework was used to synthesise the time interval between epidemic onsets and between epidemic peaks."];
  "n3" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given the induction-case that Human respiratory viruses such as influenza virus (IV) and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) are important causes of severe respiratory infections and the induction-common that Causes of severe respiratory infections require monitoring of their circulation patterns for public health preparedness, therefore human respiratory viruses are important and their seasonal patterns and resurgence need study. Currently there exist important respiratory viruses whose patterns must be understood for preparedness."];
  "n4" [label="(2,0,0) Induction-case: Previous reports showed that most of the common respiratory viruses, including IV, RSV, PIV, MPV, sCoV, and RV had distinct seasonal circulating patterns albeit with variations globally by geographical and environmental characteristics."];
  "n5" [label="(2,2,32) Induction-common: Influenza virus had clear seasonal epidemics in winter months in most temperate sites (supported by detailed global data on RSV, PIV, MPV)."];
  "n7" [label="(3,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: In 2020, the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic and large-scale implementation of non-pharmaceutical interventions (NPIs) was associated with a precipitous and major impact on the circulation of common respiratory viruses; the activity of IV and RSV remained remarkably lower during the usual circulating season in multiple countries."];
  "n8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Large-scale non-pharmaceutical interventions (NPIs) reduce transmission and activity of respiratory viruses."];
